import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Note
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The stripe package, a plugin: the package's example app file by file, developing with the Stripe
## CLI, how a payment goes, the webhook (signatures, once, out of order), who has paid, the portal,
## testing without Stripe, the tables, safety, and what comes next. Static: paying needs a server.
meta = {
	title: "Stripe",
	description: "Payments and subscriptions for Roc Solid apps through Stripe, as a plugin: hosted Checkout for one-time payments and subscriptions, the Customer Portal, a webhook that keeps the app's database in step (signed, handled once, in any order), and typed answers to who has paid for what.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "example", label: "A first app" },
		{ id: "local", label: "Developing with the Stripe CLI" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How a payment goes" },
		{ id: "webhook", label: "The webhook" },
		{ id: "paid", label: "Who has paid" },
		{ id: "testing", label: "Testing without Stripe" },
		{ id: "tables", label: "The tables" },
		{ id: "safety", label: "Safety" },
		{ id: "status", label: "Status" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/stripe</code>]}
				title="Charge from the first day"
			>
				Stripe's hosted Checkout takes a payment or starts a subscription, its Customer Portal lets people
				change their plan, their card or cancel, and a webhook keeps your app's database in step with
				Stripe. Your app asks its own tables who has paid for what. Cards are typed on Stripe's pages,
				never yours.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Charging is where small mistakes cost money: a webhook that trusts whoever posts to it, an event
				handled twice, a late event that puts an old subscription back, two customers for one person, a
				thank-you page that shows nothing because the event hasn't come yet. The package does those parts.
				Your app keeps its prices (made in Stripe's Dashboard), its pages, and <code>on_event!</code>: what it
				does itself when someone pays.
			</p>

			<Heading id="example">A first app</Heading>
			<p>
				The package has a small app, <code>packages/stripe/example</code>, that sells a subscription (Basic or
				Pro) and an e-book, and each file below is that app's. People sign in with the
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_auth}>auth package</a>'s dev sign-in. Its tests pay every way, on SQLite and
				on Postgres, against a stand-in for Stripe's API.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>1. The plugin.</strong> Name the package in the app's header and list its plugin, with your
				<code>on_event!</code>. The server needs <code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code> and <code>STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET</code>
				to start.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.stripe_example_main.file} source={Sources.stripe_example_main.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>2. Off to Checkout.</strong> A plan's button posts its price to an endpoint of yours, which calls
				<code>Stripe.checkout!</code> and redirects to the URL it gives. The mode comes from your plan, never
				from the form. The user's Stripe customer is made the first time and used every time after.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.stripe_example_checkout.file} source={Sources.stripe_example_checkout.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>3. Back from Checkout.</strong> Stripe sends the browser back as soon as it is paid, often before
				its events have come. <code>Stripe.checkout_session!</code> asks Stripe for the session (with
				<code>?session_id=</code>, which the package put in the success URL) and keeps it, so the page and
				everything after it already know.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.stripe_example_done.file} source={Sources.stripe_example_done.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>4. What your app does when someone pays.</strong> <code>on_event!</code> is given each event after
				the package has kept it, in the same transaction. Here an e-book bought becomes an order, and a
				failed renewal goes on the log:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.stripe_example_billing.file} source={Sources.stripe_example_billing.rocx} />
			<p>
				<strong>5. Pages for those who paid.</strong> Middleware asks the package's tables:
				<code>Stripe.subscribed!</code> for the subscribers' pages, <code>Stripe.paid_for!</code> for the
				e-book. Anyone else goes to the pricing page.
			</p>
			<Code title="Middlewares.roc, in part" source={Sources.stripe_example_gates.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<strong>6. The portal.</strong> A button on the account page goes to Stripe's Customer Portal, which
				comes back when they are done. What they change there comes back as events.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.stripe_example_portal.file} source={Sources.stripe_example_portal.rocx} />

			<Heading id="local">Developing with the Stripe CLI</Heading>
			<p>
				Use Stripe's test mode: its keys start <code>sk_test_</code>, nothing is charged, and its test card
				<code>4242 4242 4242 4242</code> (any future date, any CVC) pays. In the Dashboard, in test mode, make the
				products and prices you sell (their ids, <code>price_…</code>, are what <code>checkout!</code> takes), and
				save the Customer Portal's settings once: until they are saved, Stripe makes no portal sessions in
				test mode. Then forward Stripe's events to your app with the
				<a href="https://docs.stripe.com/stripe-cli">Stripe CLI</a>:
			</p>
			<Code
				source={"stripe listen --forward-to localhost:8080/stripe/webhook   # prints the webhook secret: whsec_…\nexport STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_… STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_…\nrocx dev                                                 # in another terminal\nstripe trigger checkout.session.completed                # an event of your own"}
				plain
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				In production, add a webhook endpoint in the Dashboard (Developers, Webhooks) at
				<code>{"<public URL>/stripe/webhook"}</code>, and give the server its signing secret. Send it the
				<code>checkout.session.*</code>, <code>customer.subscription.*</code>, <code>invoice.paid</code>,
				<code>invoice.payment_failed</code>, <code>customer.deleted</code>, <code>charge.refunded</code> and
				<code>charge.dispute.*</code> events: a one-time payment refunded in full, or disputed, isn't paid for
				any more (a dispute won gives it back). When the server starts it
				logs which mode its keys are, and warns about live keys under <code>--dev</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="how">How a payment goes</Heading>
			<ol>
				<li>
					Your endpoint calls <code>{"Stripe.checkout!({ user, email, mode, prices, success_url, cancel_url })"}</code>.
					The package finds the user's Stripe customer, or makes one (the app's id in its metadata), and makes a
					Checkout Session with the user as its <code>client_reference_id</code>. You redirect to its URL.
				</li>
				<li>They pay on Stripe's page. Stripe sends the browser to your success URL.</li>
				<li>
					That page calls <code>Stripe.checkout_session!</code>, which keeps what Stripe says now: the page can say
					"You're subscribed".
				</li>
				<li>
					Stripe's events arrive at the webhook: <code>checkout.session.completed</code>,
					<code>customer.subscription.created</code>, <code>invoice.paid</code>. Each is kept, and your
					<code>on_event!</code> fulfils what needs fulfilling.
				</li>
				<li>
					Later, renewals, failed payments, plan changes and cancellations in the portal arrive the same way, and
					<code>Stripe.subscribed!</code> follows them.
				</li>
			</ol>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Option of <code>checkout!</code></th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>user : Str</code></td><td>your id for whoever pays</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>email : Str</code></td><td>their address, for a customer made now (optional)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>mode : [Payment, Subscription]</code></td><td>once, or every period</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"prices : List({ price : Str, quantity : U64 })"}</code></td><td>Stripe's price ids, and how many</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>success_url</code>, <code>cancel_url</code></td><td>a path of your site, or a URL; the success URL gets <code>{"session_id={CHECKOUT_SESSION_ID}"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>trial_days : U64</code></td><td>a subscription's free days first (optional)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>promotion_codes : Bool</code></td><td>whether a promotion code may be typed (optional)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"metadata : List((Str, Str))"}</code></td><td>more of your own, on the session (optional)</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="webhook">The webhook</Heading>
			<p>
				The plugin's one endpoint, <code>POST /stripe/webhook</code> (<code>webhook_at</code>), runs behind your
				middleware. For each request:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The signature.</strong> <code>Stripe-Signature</code> must hold the HMAC-SHA256, under your
					webhook secret, of its timestamp and the body exactly as it came, and be at most five minutes old.
					Anything else is a 400. Stripe sends one signature per secret while you roll a secret; one is enough.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Once.</strong> The event's id is recorded in the transaction that handles it. One that comes
					again (Stripe sends an event until it is answered) is answered and not handled again, and two copies
					at once wait for each other.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Kept, then yours.</strong> The package keeps what the event says (a checkout's state, a
					subscription's, a deleted customer), then gives your <code>on_event!</code> the event, read: its type,
					the user it is about, and <code>Session</code>, <code>Subscription</code>, <code>Invoice</code> or
					<code>Other</code>. Its <code>Err</code> rolls everything back and answers 500, so Stripe sends the
					event again later, for up to three days.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>In any order.</strong> Stripe promises none. A subscription's row keeps the version of the event
					that last changed it, its time and its kind, so an event that comes late never puts an older state back,
					and an ended subscription stays ended. Two changes in the same second stand level, and the package asks
					Stripe which is last.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Note>
				Keep slow work out of <code>on_event!</code>: queue a <a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>job</a> for the
				receipt or the email, and it commits with the event.
			</Note>

			<Heading id="paid">Who has paid</Heading>
			<p>
				These read your database, never Stripe, so a page that checks them doesn't wait on another server, and
				nothing the browser sends decides who has paid:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Says</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.subscribed!(user)</code></td><td>whether they have a subscription that is active, trialing, or past due while Stripe tries the card again</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.paid_for!(user, what)</code></td><td>whether such a subscription has a price or product (<code>price_…</code>, <code>prod_…</code>), or a paid one-time payment was for the price and hasn't been refunded in full or disputed since</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.subscription!(user)</code></td><td>their subscription: status, items, when it renews or ends, whether it ends then</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Stripe.payments!(user)</code></td><td>their one-time payments that were paid, and not refunded in full or disputed since</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Stripe.portal!(user, return_url)"}</code></td><td>the URL of their Customer Portal, or <code>NoCustomer</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				For anything else of Stripe's API (a refund, a list of invoices), <code>Stripe.post!</code> and
				<code>Stripe.get!</code> ask it as the package does: the secret key, a pinned
				<code>Stripe-Version</code>, an idempotency key on a create, another try after a network error.
				That key is new each call; for a create the app may ask twice (a job run again, a form sent twice),
				<code>Stripe.post_with!</code> takes a key of the app's own, so Stripe makes it once.
			</p>

			<Heading id="testing">Testing without Stripe</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>STRIPE_API_URL</code> points the package at another server. The example's tests start a stand-in
				for Stripe's API (<code>example/test/stand-in.mjs</code>, Node with no dependencies): the endpoints the
				package calls, answering with Stripe's objects, and what paying on Checkout's page would do. Each test
				makes an event and signs it as Stripe does, and sends it to the webhook. Copy it into your app's tests.
			</p>

			<Heading id="tables">The tables</Heading>
			<p>
				The plugin's migrations (<code>stripe/0001_stripe</code>, then <code>stripe/0002_refunds</code>) make four
				tables, the same on SQLite and on Postgres:
				<code>stripe_customers</code> (each user's Stripe customer), <code>stripe_checkouts</code>,
				<code>stripe_subscriptions</code> (as the events last said) and <code>stripe_events</code> (those handled,
				swept after 30 days). Your users stay in your own table; the package knows them by your id.
			</p>

			<Heading id="safety">Safety</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>No card reaches your server: Checkout and the portal are Stripe's pages.</li>
				<li>The webhook takes signed events only, compared in constant time, at most five minutes old.</li>
				<li>A checkout belongs to its user: the success page refuses another user's session.</li>
				<li>Every create carries an idempotency key, so a request tried again makes one customer, one session.</li>
				<li>Test and live keys are told apart at start, and live keys under <code>--dev</code> are a warning.</li>
			</ul>
			<Note>
				A middleware that wants every request signed in would refuse Stripe's webhook too. The webhook has no
				<code>Origin</code>, so <code>Middleware.same_origin</code> lets it through.
			</Note>

			<Heading id="status">Status</Heading>
			<p>
				Checkout (payments and subscriptions, trials, promotion codes), the Customer Portal, the webhook, the
				checks for who has paid, and refunds and disputes taking a one-time payment back are built and
				tested. Stripe Tax, metered billing, Connect and embedded Checkout are not; the package's
				<code>PROPOSALS.md</code> has them.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
